        {
          "ruleId": "gate.mutants-cuda-rule-inert",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "F1 (RESOLVED IN DIFF, not open). Round 1 (head d07e187b66) found that the gate rule at ci.yml:483-484 read `.outputs.not_measured` / `.outputs.measured_sha` from the results job outputs while scripts/ci/fat_driver.py emit_results_output dropped `outputs`, so the rule read empty and passed on every run. Head fc88268134 (one commit, scripts only) fixes it at the source: emit_results_output now writes `outputs` per section (fat_driver.py:1245-1248), adds a fat_driver self-test row that the emitted results keep a section's outputs, adds scripts/ci/emit_results_sample.py, and adds row 14 to check_ci_gate_mutants_cuda_rule.sh that feeds the gate rule the driver's own emission instead of hand-written JSON. Re-verified independently, by measurement and not by reading the diff: (a) the old driver blob run through the same sample emits {result,continue_on_error} only and the gate reads not_measured='' ; the new driver emits the outputs and the gate reads not_measured='3'; (b) the rule script's new row 14 run against a scratch tree carrying the OLD fat_driver.py FAILS ('driver-emitted results carry no outputs: the rule read them as empty and passed'), and against the new driver passes (RED as required); (c) fat_driver.py self-test 53/53, check_ci_gate_mutants_cuda_rule.sh PASS (14 rows), --self-test PASS; (d) two hand-made mutants of the fix (drop the key; emit a constant {}) are each killed by the new fat_driver self-test row. Both producers exist and feed the keys the rule reads: ci/sections.yml:3437-3439 (not_measured, not_measured_sha) and :3174-3176 (measured_sha). Residual, not blocking: row 14 asserts only the exit status of the rule block, not which branch made it RED; it discriminates here only because the old driver turns it green."
          },
          "locations": [
