      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "cuda.ptx-isa-confirmed",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Queried: PTX bfe.u32 bit field extract zero-extend semantics; selp.u32 conditional select semantics. Result: found for bfe (PTX ISA 9.7.1.20): for .u32 the sign bit is zero, so the extracted field is zero-extended, and a zero length gives zero. Iq4XsDequantKernel in f16_iq4xs_dequant.rs uses bfe.u32 with start and length inside 0..255, and zero-extension is what a 4-bit nibble and 6-bit scale extraction need. The corpus returned no selp section for this query; selp correctness rests on the file's bitwise-parity test against the CPU reader, not on a citation."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "crates/aprender-gpu/src/kernels/quantize/f16_iq4xs_dequant.rs"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 1,
                  "endLine": 285
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "cited",
            "source": "NVIDIA PTX ISA, Integer Arithmetic Instructions: bfe (section 9.7.1.20), via mcp__nvidia-cuda-docs__search_cuda_docs",
            "excerpt": "Extract bit field from `a` and place the zero or sign-extended result in `d`. Source `b` gives the bit field starting bit position, and source `c` gives the bit field length in bits. The sign bit of the extracted field is defined as: `.u32`, `.u64`: zero. If the bit field length is zero, the result is zero.",
            "excerpt_sha256": "63076f33f668515991ff2d079daca7852eb7c10da4431beb65523e0a2272aeb3",
            "failure_scenario": "N/A - confirms instruction semantics; if bfe were sign-extending, scale nibbles would go negative and the dequantized weights would flip sign."
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "cuda.graph-param-baking-confirmed",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Queried: CUDA graph replay kernel node parameters baked at capture; updating parameters. Result: found. CUDA Programming Guide 4.2.3: kernel parameters and memory addresses are per-node state of the instantiated graph and change only through the Graph Update mechanism, taking effect on the next launch. That supports graph_may_run (prefill.rs:1598): a graph captured for positions 0..S-1 cannot serve a later chunk whose positions and KV length differ, so only a chunk starting at position 0 may replay it (call site prefill.rs:167)."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "crates/aprender-serve/src/cuda/executor/layers/prefill.rs"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 1598,
                  "endLine": 1600
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "cited",
            "source": "NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide, section 4.2.3 Updating Instantiated Graphs, via mcp__nvidia-cuda-docs__search_cuda_docs",
            "excerpt": "It is common for only node parameters (such as kernel parameters and memory addresses) to change while the graph topology remains the same. For this case, CUDA provides a lightweight Graph Update mechanism that allows certain node parameters to be modified in-place without rebuilding the entire graph. Updates take effect the next time the graph is launched.",
            "excerpt_sha256": "2dfb426eb82917de6afd6249cbbd8828352371aa3d8526c2847d40f244cdfe88",
            "failure_scenario": "Without graph_may_run, chunk 2 of a multi-chunk prompt with PREFILL_GRAPH=1 replays the chunk-1 graph and overwrites the KV it just extended."
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "cuda.no-authority-found",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Queried: IQ4_XS ggml quantization block layout 136 bytes superblock 256 values 6-bit scale. Result: no-authority-found; the corpus returned CUTLASS block-scaled scale-factor layouts, which are unrelated. IQ4_XS is a ggml format, so the docs consultation has no authority over it; the only evidence is the file's own bitwise-parity test against the CPU reader (f16_iq4xs_dequant.rs)."
          },
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "asserted",
            "precision_class": "advisory",
            "failure_scenario": "N/A - records a gap in this consultation's authority, not a defect."
          }
        }
      ],
      "invocations": [
        {
          "executionSuccessful": true
        }
